      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2 id="corporate-rate" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">1. Singapore Corporate Tax Overview: The Power of 17%</h2>
    <p>Administered by the Inland Revenue Authority of Singapore (<a href="https://www.iras.gov.sg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-link hover:underline">IRAS</a>), corporate income tax in Singapore is levied on a territorial basis. This means that tax applies to all income accruing in or derived from Singapore, as well as foreign-sourced income that is remitted into Singapore.</p>
    
    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-6">Why the Territorial System Matters</h3>
    <p>Unlike countries that tax worldwide income regardless of where it is earned, Singapore’s territorial system offers a massive advantage for international businesses. If your Singapore company earns revenue from operations overseas and that income is never remitted into Singapore (meaning it is kept in foreign bank accounts and used for foreign expenses), it may not be subject to Singapore tax at all.</p>
    
    <p>Furthermore, even if you do remit foreign-sourced income (such as branch profits, foreign dividends, or foreign service income) into Singapore, you may still be exempt from tax under Section 13(8) of the Income Tax Act, provided that the foreign jurisdiction has a headline tax rate of at least 15% and the income has already been subjected to some form of tax overseas. This practically eliminates double taxation.</p>

    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-6">Effective Tax Rates vs. Headline Tax Rates</h3>
    <p>While the headline rate is 17%, the reality for most Small and Medium Enterprises (SMEs) is entirely different. Due to partial tax exemptions and various schemes, the <em>effective corporate tax rate</em> for companies with taxable profits up to S$200,000 often falls below 8% to 10%. This distinction is critical for business planning. Don't base your cash flow projections on a flat 17% if you qualify for these exemptions.</p>

    <h2 id="startup-exemption" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">2. Startup Tax Exemption (SOTE) Scheme</h2>
    <p>Starting a new business is inherently risky, and capital preservation in the early years is crucial to survival. To fiercely support entrepreneurship and attract global talent, the Singapore government introduced the <em>Startup Tax Exemption Scheme (SOTE)</em>. This scheme provides significant tax relief for qualifying new Singapore private limited companies during their first three consecutive Years of Assessment (YA).</p>

    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-6">Qualifying Conditions for SOTE</h3>
    <p>We often have clients assume they automatically get this exemption just by incorporating. However, you must meet three strict criteria:</p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 mt-4 mb-4">
      <li><strong>Incorporation:</strong> The company must be incorporated in Singapore.</li>
      <li><strong>Tax Residency:</strong> The company must be a tax resident in Singapore for that Year of Assessment.</li>
      <li><strong>Shareholding Structure:</strong> The total share capital is beneficially held directly by no more than 20 shareholders throughout the basis period. Furthermore, all shareholders must be individuals, OR at least one shareholder must be an individual holding at least 10% of the issued ordinary shares.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><em>Note: Investment holding companies and companies that undertake property development for sale or investment are explicitly excluded from the SOTE scheme.</em></p>

    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-6">How the SOTE Exemption is Calculated</h3>
    <p>The exemptions are applied in tiers against the company's normal chargeable income:</p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-4">
      <table class="min-w-full border border-hairline text-left text-sm dark:border-overlay-lighter">
        <thead class="bg-canvas-soft font-semibold text-ink dark:bg-surface dark:text-on-primary">
